# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
more_disgraceful	one
exceeds_pain	two	pain	P3	exceeds in pain caused, as the standard measures disgrace (pain to those affected or looking on)	exceeds in pain felt by the doer himself (do injurers suffer more than the injured)
exceeds_evil	one
more_evil	one
bridge	P5	evil	evil as harm or badness produced by the act, the standard that measures disgrace	evil as what is worse for the one who does or undergoes it, the sense of Polus's claim that suffering wrong is more evil
# The pain standard comes from the account of beauty (S1, S20), where beauty is measured by pleasure to spectators. So the disgrace of an act should be measured by the pain it causes to others.
# S22 tests instead whether the injurers themselves suffer more than the injured.
# Less sure: whether "exceeds in evil" already carries the "bad for the agent" sense in P2. I placed the shift at P5, where the excess of evil that makes an act disgraceful becomes the act being a greater evil in Polus's sense (P1, C).
